# GCP project Starfish runs in: its Firestore store. Gemini also runs here unless
# STARFISH_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID says otherwise (enable the Vertex AI API in whichever it is).
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=

# GCP project whose Cloud Logging is read for errors. Unset: GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT
# (Starfish watching the project it runs in). One watched project per deployment:
# change STARFISH_ENV with it so two projects' incidents never share a label.
# STARFISH_WATCHED_PROJECT_ID=

# GCP project Gemini runs in (and is billed to). Unset: GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT. Set it to
# run Gemini in a different project from Starfish's store.
# STARFISH_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID=

# Vertex AI region.
GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION=us-central1

# Gemini model used for triage, fix drafting and adversarial review.
STARFISH_MODEL=gemini-3.1-pro-preview

# Label stamped on every incident. Environments must never share a value —
# this is what stops a dev incident from being treated as a production one.
STARFISH_ENV=dev

# Firestore collection holding incidents, and the database holding it. Starfish uses
# its own named database ("starfish") so its rules and indexes never touch your app's;
# create it once: gcloud firestore databases create --database=starfish --location=<region>
# "(default)" opts back into the default database.
STARFISH_INCIDENT_COLLECTION=incidents
# Collection holding the runtime settings document an admin edits from the UI. The
# values in this file are the defaults; a valid settings document overrides the
# watched project, environment label, log resource types, service patterns and
# noise list. Unset: starfish_settings.
# STARFISH_SETTINGS_COLLECTION=starfish_settings

# Engine-only collection mapping each (environment, fingerprint) to its incident, so
# concurrent ingest runs converge on one document. Unset: incident_keys.
# STARFISH_INCIDENT_KEY_COLLECTION=incident_keys
# STARFISH_DATABASE_ID=starfish

# Substring -> service attribution, "pattern:service,pattern:service".
# A function matching nothing resolves to "platform" rather than being
# attributed to whichever service happens to be listed first.
STARFISH_SERVICE_PATTERNS=

# How far back the first aggregation run looks when no checkpoint exists.
STARFISH_LOOKBACK_HOURS=24

# The command that proves a proposed patch is acceptable. It runs in a scratch copy of the target
# repo (without .git) with the patch applied; exit 0 is the only pass. Unset: no patch is ever
# accepted. Runs with a scrubbed environment, but it is NOT a sandbox: run it on a disposable runner.
# STARFISH_VERIFY_COMMAND="pnpm install --frozen-lockfile && pnpm run verify"
# STARFISH_VERIFY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
# Paths a patch may never touch, added to the built-in list (.git/, .github/, .claude/, .env*,
# keys and credential files). Comma-separated; a trailing "/" is a directory, "*" and "**" are globs,
# a pattern with no "/" matches that name at any depth. List the files that define the verify
# command and the tests that judge it (package.json scripts, test config), or a patch can edit the judge.
# STARFISH_PATCH_EXCLUDED_PATHS=

# Ingest windows end this many minutes behind real time, because Cloud Logging makes an
# entry searchable a little after it happened. Unset: 5.
# STARFISH_INGEST_LAG_MINUTES=5
# The longest span one window covers; catching up after downtime is done in windows of this
# size. Unset: 6.
# STARFISH_INGEST_MAX_WINDOW_HOURS=6
# The Cloud Scheduler cron that starts the ingest, so the UI can say when the next run is expected
# (scripts/deploy-gcp.sh and scripts/ingest-cadence.sh set it). Unset: the UI says nothing about the next run.
# STARFISH_INGEST_SCHEDULE="0 * * * *"
# Functions and services whose errors are never ingested, comma-separated, matched ignoring case and
# with `_` read as `-`. Put Starfish's own Cloud Run service or job name here: when it shares a
# project with the app it watches, it would otherwise read its own errors and the fixer could be
# asked to patch Starfish itself. A trailing * excludes a prefix (`control-*`, `stub-*`). Unset: starfish. Set to empty to exclude nothing (not advised).
# STARFISH_EXCLUDED_SERVICES=starfish

# Operator records, which the engine reads to check that an admin action really comes from an
# admin, and the audit trail of those actions. Unset: starfish_users, starfish_audit. The rules
# name both, like the other collections.
# STARFISH_USERS_COLLECTION=starfish_users
# STARFISH_AUDIT_COLLECTION=starfish_audit

# Dry run: the agent loop drafts and verifies everything and opens nothing. ON by default, so
# nothing leaves Starfish until you set this to false on purpose. REVIEW_REQUIRED: a patch must
# survive the adversarial review before it can become a pull request. MODEL_CALL_BUDGET: the most
# model calls one cycle may make (triage, each fix attempt, each review).
# STARFISH_DRY_RUN=true
# STARFISH_REVIEW_REQUIRED=true
# The kill switch: false stops the agent loop at its next step. Unset: true. An admin can also flip it on the
# Settings page, and it is read fresh before every stage (src/agent/automationSwitch.ts).
# STARFISH_AUTOMATION_ENABLED=true
# The agent entrypoint (`pnpm agent`, src/agentCli.ts, #74) refuses to start unless this is set. none (the
# default): the verify command runs nowhere, so the agent cannot run. in-process: it runs the target repo's
# own tests on THIS machine as THIS identity: not a sandbox. Use it only on a machine with no credentials the
# tested code must not reach (a laptop with none set, not the engine's Cloud Run job). MAX_CYCLES_PER_PASS:
# the most cycles one pass starts. Unset: none, 3.
# cloud-run-job: the verify command runs in a SEPARATE Cloud Run job whose service account holds no roles; the
# agent packs the source and patch into STARFISH_VERIFY_BUCKET, starts STARFISH_VERIFY_JOB (in
# STARFISH_VERIFY_JOB_REGION, unset: GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION) with per-run signed URLs, and waits for the result
# (docs/deploy.md). scripts/deploy-gcp.sh sets all three with DEPLOY_AGENT=1.
# github-actions: the patch is pushed to a starfish/<cycle> branch in STARFISH_GITHUB_REPO and the TARGET's own
# CI, on GitHub's disposable runners, is the judge: STARFISH_VERIFY_CHECKS names the check runs that must all pass.
# The agent never runs the target's code. The target's workflow must run on `push` to `starfish/**` and should hold
# no secrets (a patch's code runs in it); a branch is pushed even in a dry run (docs/deploy.md).
# STARFISH_VERIFY_ISOLATION=none
# STARFISH_VERIFY_CHECKS=
# STARFISH_VERIFY_JOB=
# STARFISH_VERIFY_BUCKET=
# STARFISH_VERIFY_JOB_REGION=
# STARFISH_MAX_CYCLES_PER_PASS=3
# STARFISH_MODEL_CALL_BUDGET=6
# The agent loop works one cycle per incident and recurrence. A worker holds a lease on its
# cycle for this long before another may take it over (a crashed worker's cycle is resumed);
# the cycle as a whole has a deadline the lease can never extend, and is given up on after
# this many starts. Collection holds the cycles and their run events. Unset: 300 s, 60 min, 3.
# STARFISH_CYCLE_COLLECTION=starfish_cycles
# STARFISH_LEASE_SECONDS=300
# STARFISH_CYCLE_DEADLINE_MINUTES=60
# STARFISH_MAX_CYCLE_ATTEMPTS=3
# Collection for the ingest checkpoint and the last-run record the UI shows. Unset: starfish_runs.
# STARFISH_RUN_COLLECTION=starfish_runs

# The pull-request step (src/integrations/githubPr.ts). It opens pull requests in ONE repository,
# owner/name, from a branch named starfish/<cycle id> that it pushes (never force, never the base
# branch); it never merges. Empty repo: nothing can be opened. The token is a secret and is read
# only from STARFISH_GITHUB_TOKEN (Secret Manager on Cloud Run, docs/deploy.md), never from a
# setting. Limiting the token to starfish/* branches is a branch-protection / ruleset job on the
# repository: the engine checks it too, but a GitHub App cannot be scoped to a branch prefix.
# The same repo and token also file ONE issue per incident the agent acts on or escalates (label "starfish",
# links via STARFISH_UI_URL), so the token needs Issues: read and write too. A dry run files nothing.
# Unset: no repo, base branch main, https://api.github.com, https://github.com/<repo>.git,
# commits by "Starfish <starfish@users.noreply.github.com>".
# STARFISH_GITHUB_REPO=owner/name
# STARFISH_GITHUB_BASE_BRANCH=main
# STARFISH_GITHUB_API_URL=https://api.github.com
# STARFISH_GITHUB_REMOTE_URL=
# STARFISH_GITHUB_COMMIT_NAME=Starfish
# STARFISH_GITHUB_COMMIT_EMAIL=starfish@users.noreply.github.com
# STARFISH_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Slack (src/integrations/slack.ts): when a cycle ends waiting for a person (triage said notify; the fixer
# declined; the verify gate or the review would not pass a second time), the agent posts ONE message to ONE
# channel through an incoming webhook, with links back to the incident and its run. The webhook URL is a
# secret (anyone holding it can post to the channel): read only from STARFISH_SLACK_WEBHOOK_ONCALL (Secret
# Manager on Cloud Run, docs/deploy.md), never a setting, and refused unless it is a hooks.slack.com URL.
# STARFISH_UI_URL is the operator UI's origin (https://<site>.web.app) for those links; unset: no links.
# In a dry run (STARFISH_DRY_RUN, the default) nothing is sent and the cycle's record says so.
# STARFISH_SLACK_WEBHOOK_ONCALL=
# STARFISH_UI_URL=

# Cloud Logging resource types whose ERROR entries count, comma-separated.
# Unset: cloud_function,cloud_run_revision.
# STARFISH_LOG_RESOURCE_TYPES=

# ERROR-severity messages that are framework noise, "|"-separated. Unset keeps
# the callable-framework GET-probe defaults; set to empty to suppress nothing.
# STARFISH_IGNORED_MESSAGES=

# Missing-index heal (#11, src/indexheal/createIndexUrl.ts): the project/database pairs a create-index URL
# may be acted on for, comma-separated, e.g. my-demo-project/(default). Empty (the default) allows nothing.
# Starfish's own database is refused even if listed.
# STARFISH_INDEX_ALLOWED_TARGETS=
# Creating the index is its own switch, off by default (and `autoMerge` never turns it on). It also needs
# STARFISH_DRY_RUN=false and the automation switch on. At most STARFISH_INDEX_DAILY_CAP are created per target per UTC day.
# STARFISH_INDEX_AUTO_APPLY=false
# STARFISH_INDEX_DAILY_CAP=3

# Admin API (src/adminApi.ts, #63, #65): the operator UI's origin(s), comma-separated, allowed to call it from a
# browser, e.g. https://my-site.web.app. Exact https origins only (http://localhost is accepted for local work).
# Empty (the default): no browser may; a server-side caller (the demo's reset script) is not affected by CORS.
# The server itself listens on PORT (Cloud Run sets it; default 8080).
# STARFISH_ADMIN_API_ORIGINS=
